<section class="content-section">
  <h2>Where and When EET and EST are Used</h2>
  <p>
    Both time zones play critical roles across international commerce, infrastructure, and culture.
  </p>
  <div class="use-cases">
    <article>
      <h3>Eastern European Time (EET) Usage</h3>
      <p>
        EET is the standard time zone covering Eastern European Union member states and neighboring nations. Key countries observing EET include Greece, Finland, Romania, Bulgaria, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, and Ukraine. In addition, Egypt operates on UTC+2 standard time. Key metropolitan centers include Athens, Helsinki, Bucharest, Sofia, Tallinn, and Riga.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Eastern Standard Time (EST) Usage</h3>
      <p>
        EST governs civil time for the eastern portion of the United States and Canada, along with several Caribbean islands and Central American nations. Major cities in this zone include New York City, Washington D.C., Boston, Toronto, Montreal, Miami, and Atlanta. EST is the benchmark zone for US financial markets and federal government communications.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Cross-Continental Commerce</h3>
      <p>
        The overlap between Eastern European technology centers (such as Bucharest's IT hub, Tallinn's fintech ecosystem, and Helsinki's game development studios) and US capital markets requires continuous time zone translation between EET and EST.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     COMMON MISTAKES SECTION
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="content-section">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Converting EET to EST</h2>
  <div class="use-cases">
    <article>
      <h3>1. Forgetting the Calendar Day Line</h3>
      <p>
        When converting early morning EET times (such as 1:00 AM – 6:00 AM EET), subtracting 7 hours moves the time back into the previous calendar evening in EST. Failing to note this date shift can cause meetings to be scheduled on the wrong day.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>2. Confusing EET with CET or GMT</h3>
      <p>
        Eastern European Time (UTC+2) is 1 hour ahead of Central European Time (CET, UTC+1) and 2 hours ahead of Greenwich Mean Time (GMT, UTC+0). Applying a 5-hour or 6-hour offset instead of 7 hours is a frequent conversion error.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>3. Miscalculating Spring & Autumn DST Discrepancies</h3>
      <p>
        Because the US and Europe switch daylight saving clocks on different Sundays in March and October, assuming the gap is always 7 hours year-round will lead to scheduling errors during those transition weeks.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

